{
 "about": "Each playable realm's state in 300 BC. units: [heavy infantry (1,000), light infantry (1,000), archers and slingers (1,000), cavalry (500), horse archers (500), war elephants (20), warships (10)]. Sizes follow the field armies and fleets of c. 301-280 BC (Ipsus, the Pyrrhic War, the early Syrian Wars; Diodorus, Plutarch, Polybius); tribal peoples count their fighting men who could gather. treasury_years: starting silver as years of tax income. elephant_source: can raise elephants without holding elephant country (the Seleucids had Chandragupta's 500 and the Indian trade). manpower: how many times the usual share of people a realm can call up (Rome's Italian alliance; tribal levies). army_share: share of income spent on the army in peace (default 0.5; Rome, a society organised for war, more). upkeep_share: share of the army's upkeep the realm pays itself (Rome's Italian allies, the socii, fed and paid their own contingents, about half of every Roman army: Polybius 2.24, 6.26). alliances: treaties in force in 300 BC (the victors of Ipsus, 301 BC: Seleucus and Lysimachus; Ptolemy's league with Lysimachus, sealed by Arsinoe's marriage in 300/299 BC). rivals: history's great rivalries, which colour how realms see each other (Rome against Carthage, the Samnites, the Etruscans and the Gauls; the Ptolemies and Seleucids over Syria; Demetrios against the other Successors; Carthage and Agathokles of Sicily; Tarentum and its Italic neighbours; Lysimachos and the Thracians, Getae and Bithynians; Sparta and Macedon; the Scythians and the Sarmatians). Egypt also stood with Pyrrhos and Agathokles, whom Ptolemy married to his stepdaughters.",
